<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p><strong>Self-study quiz.</strong> This quiz is for your own review — answers are not recorded. You’ll get instant feedback on each question.</p>
</div></div>

<div class="quiz"><div class="quiz__top-bar"><div class="quiz__score-tracker" style="display:none;"></div><button class="quiz__start-over" type="button" style="display:none;">Start Over</button></div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q1c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">1</span> According to this unit, which of the following is NOT one of the conceptual criteria for identifying something as art?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1a" id="q1a">
        <label for="q1a">It was intended as art.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1b" id="q1b">
        <label for="q1b">It was made by an artist.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1c" id="q1c">
        <label for="q1c">It is beautiful.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1d" id="q1d">
        <label for="q1d">Experts in the field accept it as art.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The conceptual approach asks who made it, whether it was intended as art, and whether experts agree. Beauty is not one of the criteria, and many works of art are not beautiful.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The conceptual criteria concern who made the work, what they intended, and whether experts accept it. Look for the quality that is not on that list.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q2d">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">2</span> Why is Marcel Duchamp's Fountain a hard case for the conceptual criteria?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2a" id="q2a">
        <label for="q2a">It is clearly not a work of art because it is a plumbing fixture.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2b" id="q2b">
        <label for="q2b">Experts have always agreed that it is a masterpiece.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2c" id="q2c">
        <label for="q2c">It satisfies every criterion in the most ordinary way.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2d" id="q2d">
        <label for="q2d">Duchamp did not make the urinal, yet by choosing and signing it he presented it as art, which challenges what it means for an artist to make something.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Fountain pushes on every criterion: who is the artist if the object was manufactured, what counts as intention, and who decides? That is why it is still argued about.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Think about the three criteria: authorship, intention, expert agreement. Fountain tests each one, which is why it is a hard case, not a simple example.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q3d">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">3</span> The perceptual approach to identifying art asks</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3a" id="q3a">
        <label for="q3a">how much it cost.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3b" id="q3b">
        <label for="q3b">what kind of thing it is.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3c" id="q3c">
        <label for="q3c">who owns it.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3d" id="q3d">
        <label for="q3d">what the thing does when you actually look at it, listen to it, or experience it.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The conceptual approach asks what kind of thing something is. The perceptual approach asks what it does to the person experiencing it.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The two approaches ask different questions. The conceptual one is about what kind of thing it is; the perceptual one is about what happens when you experience it.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q4b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">4</span> Exekias's black-figure amphora was a useful storage jar, but its figures of Achilles and Ajax are carefully composed. What does this show about identifying art perceptually?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4a" id="q4a">
        <label for="q4a">Artistic form depends on how old an object is.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4b" id="q4b">
        <label for="q4b">Usefulness does not prevent artistic form; what matters is that the parts are unified so the work rewards sustained attention.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4c" id="q4c">
        <label for="q4c">Useful objects cannot be art.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4d" id="q4d">
        <label for="q4d">Only objects made for museums have artistic form.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Perceptually, the question is what the object does: the lines of the warriors' bodies and the table are arranged so closely that you want to keep looking.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Look at the Gallery image again. The jar had a practical use, but the figures are arranged with great care. Ask what that care does for the person looking.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q5a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">5</span> Francisco Goya's The Third of May 1808 and Eddie Adams's photograph Execution in Saigon share a subject. What does comparing them show about artistic form?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5a" id="q5a">
        <label for="q5a">Artistic form is a matter of degree: Goya has composed his scene, while Adams captured a real moment.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5b" id="q5b">
        <label for="q5b">The two works have exactly the same amount of artistic form.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5c" id="q5c">
        <label for="q5c">Artistic form only exists in paintings.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5d" id="q5d">
        <label for="q5d">Photographs can never be art.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Goya's lighting, faceless firing squad, and converging lines are composed. The photograph captures a real event. That does not stop it from being art; it means recognizing degrees of form matters.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The lesson does not say the photograph is not art. It says artistic form comes in degrees, and Goya's painting has been composed to a much higher degree.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q6a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">6</span> A participative experience involves</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6a" id="q6a">
        <label for="q6a">undivided and sustained attention to a work of art.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6b" id="q6b">
        <label for="q6b">knowing the artist's biography.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6c" id="q6c">
        <label for="q6c">discussing a work with other people.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6d" id="q6d">
        <label for="q6d">identifying with a character.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Participation is the experience of losing track of yourself and your surroundings because a work holds your attention completely.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Participation is not about other people or about characters. It is about attention that is undivided and sustained, so that you forget where you are.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q7b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">7</span> Which of these best describes a participative experience?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7a" id="q7a">
        <label for="q7a">Telling a friend which songs are popular.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7b" id="q7b">
        <label for="q7b">Reading a novel so closely that you forget where you are.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7c" id="q7c">
        <label for="q7c">Checking a painting's date in the museum label.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7d" id="q7d">
        <label for="q7d">Deciding quickly that you do not like a film.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. When a work holds you so fully that your sense of time disappears, you are participating in it.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Participation is about being absorbed. Look for the example in which attention is so complete that the person loses track of their surroundings.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q8b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">8</span> Subject matter and content are different because</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8a" id="q8a">
        <label for="q8a">they are two words for the same thing.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8b" id="q8b">
        <label for="q8b">subject matter is what a work is about, while content is that subject matter clarified and made meaningful through artistic form.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8c" id="q8c">
        <label for="q8c">subject matter is always beautiful.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8d" id="q8d">
        <label for="q8d">content is always the title of the work.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The subject matter of a war painting is war; its content is what the artist's form makes of that subject: what the war is like, what it costs.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The two terms are not synonyms. Subject matter is the topic; content is the topic as shaped, clarified, and made meaningful by form.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q9a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">9</span> In Bruegel's Landscape with the Fall of Icarus, the subject matter is suffering, but the content is indifference. How does the painting produce that content?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9a" id="q9a">
        <label for="q9a">Through form: Icarus is tiny and peripheral while the rest of the painting is filled with people going about their work.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9b" id="q9b">
        <label for="q9b">Through a caption that explains that people ignored Icarus.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9c" id="q9c">
        <label for="q9c">Through the painting's size alone.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9d" id="q9d">
        <label for="q9d">Through the title only.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Nothing in the painting says so in words. The meaning is in the scale, placement, and relationships between the figures. That is what fusing meaning into form looks like.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The lesson stresses that the content is not stated anywhere. Look for the answer that locates the meaning in the painting's composition.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q10c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">10</span> James Turrell's Twilight Epiphany, a skyspace, is described as participation in its purest form. Why?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10a" id="q10a">
        <label for="q10a">It depends on a title to explain it.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10b" id="q10b">
        <label for="q10b">It tells a detailed story about the artist's life.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10c" id="q10c">
        <label for="q10c">It does not represent anything else; it creates the conditions for sustained, undivided attention to the sky itself.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10d" id="q10d">
        <label for="q10d">It makes an argument about politics.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The skyspace makes no argument and tells no story. It simply holds your attention so fully that you lose track of yourself.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Think about what the skyspace asks of you. It is not a story or an argument; it is an experience of sustained attention.</div>
  </div>
</div>

